            <ArticleTitle>Stenting and surgery for coronary vasospasm : the wrong solution fails to solve the problem.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>564-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00059-009-3228-3</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 55-year-old man, with a history of medically uncontrolled coronary vasospasm, presented for evaluation of chest pain 6 months after implantation of left internal mammary artery. Due to recurrent episodes of vasospastic angina and serious complications of coronary spasm (ventricular fibrillation, myocardial infarction), a stent had previously been implanted in the proximal part of left anterior descending artery at the site of angiographically and ergonovine-proven coronary spasm, with subsequent in-stent restenosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
